You are working in the GreenEarthX Platform v6.1 codebase.

Communication
- Keep answers structured and concise.
- Keep code comments as short anchor points, not narration.
- State assumptions clearly before making architecture-impacting changes.

Scope
- Treat this directory as the canonical GEX application.
- Do not try to sync edits to sibling copies such as `co-work/` unless the user explicitly asks.
- For finance-engine-backed behavior, treat `../gex_pf_engine/backend` as part of the effective system boundary even though it sits outside this folder.

Project map
- Frontend: `frontend` using Vite, React 18, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, React Router, TanStack Query.
- Backend: `backend` using FastAPI and SQLite.
- Finance engine integration is proxied through the platform backend into `../gex_pf_engine/backend` on port `8001`.
- `../gex_pf_engine/backend` owns core bankability, financial-model, and pricing engine behavior; the platform backend owns browser-facing orchestration, evidence persistence, and proxy routes.

Architecture rules
- Preserve the v6.1 navigation model. `frontend/src/config/menuArchitecture.ts` is the single source of truth for menu tabs, items, sections, and visibility rules.
- Preserve role semantics in `frontend/src/contexts/UserRoleContext.tsx`: `company_type`, `business_function`, `service_type`, `capabilities`, and `sessionTier`.
- When adding a user-facing page, update route registration in `frontend/src/App.tsx` and update `menuArchitecture.ts` when the page belongs in navigation.
- Treat the finance-engine boundary as explicit architecture:
  - `backend/app/api/v1/routes_bankability_proxy.py` proxies to `../gex_pf_engine/backend/app/api/routes_bankability.py`.
  - `backend/app/api/v1/routes_finance_model.py` proxies to `../gex_pf_engine/backend/app/api/routes_model.py`.
  - Platform evidence and snapshots stay in the platform backend; the 12-gate evaluation logic stays in `../gex_pf_engine/backend/app/core/bankability_engine.py`.
  - If a change affects contract shape, gate semantics, state transitions, or model/pricing behavior, inspect and update both sides.
- Respect product workflow semantics:
  - Verification states remain `UNVERIFIED`, `SUBMITTED`, `CONFIRMED`, `AUDITED`.
  - Deal-killers remain non-dismissable and `FATAL` blockers must keep blocking committee, FID, and export flows.
  - Prefer decision-first workflows over decorative score-first dashboards.
- Optimize for enterprise green-fuels workflows: bankability, compliance, traceability, approvals, operations, and auditability.

Platform vs engine decision rule
- Edit `frontend` when the change is UI, routing, interaction flow, role-filtered visibility, or client-side state only.
- Edit `backend` when the change is orchestration, auth/session behavior, evidence persistence, audit/provenance, approvals/CISO policy, proxying, or browser-facing API shaping.
- Edit `../gex_pf_engine/backend` when the change is bankability gate logic, persona evaluation, state-machine behavior, capital unlocks, CFADS, covenants, waterfalls, pricing curves, or finance math.
- Edit both sides when the change crosses `/api/v1/bankability/*`, `/api/v1/finance-model/*`, or any engine-backed request/response contract.
- If uncertain, keep business math and evaluation logic in `../gex_pf_engine/backend`, and keep persistence, orchestration, and user/workspace concerns in `backend`.
- Do not patch an engine-side defect by hardcoding compensating logic in `frontend` or `backend` unless the user explicitly wants a temporary workaround.

Frontend conventions
- Follow strict TypeScript. Prefer explicit interfaces and types. Avoid `any` unless there is no practical alternative.
- Use `@/` imports where possible.
- Keep domain code inside the relevant `frontend/src/features/*` area. Promote only shared logic or shared UI to `components`, `hooks`, or `lib`.
- Prefer reusable hooks and shared API helpers over adding new inline `fetch()` calls inside page components.
- Favor existing API patterns in `frontend/src/api.ts`, `frontend/src/lib/`, or feature hooks. Do not create duplicate "old", "copy", or parallel API modules.
- Avoid hardcoded backend hosts in frontend code. Prefer relative `/api/...` paths via the Vite proxy or a centralized helper. Use direct `:8001` calls only when the feature already depends on them and the reason is explicit.
- Match the existing Tailwind-first visual language unless the user asks for a redesign. Keep labels task-oriented and plain-language.

Backend conventions
- Add new platform routes under `backend/app/api/v1/` or `backend/app/api/endpoints/`.
- Register new routers in `backend/app/main.py`.
- Keep domain logic in `backend/app/core/` or `backend/app/services/`, not in route handlers.
- Do not rename or move existing API paths that the frontend consumes unless the change is coordinated end-to-end.
- For engine-owned finance logic, change `../gex_pf_engine/backend/app/core/` or `../gex_pf_engine/backend/app/api/` rather than duplicating behavior in the platform backend.
- Keep port `8000` as the browser-facing layer and port `8001` as the engine layer. Do not move frontend traffic directly to engine endpoints unless the user explicitly requests that architecture change.
- When engine contracts change, update the engine route/core logic, the platform proxy, and the consuming frontend/backend caller together.

Quality bar
- Inspect nearby modules first and mirror the local pattern instead of imposing a new architecture.
- For frontend changes, validate with `cd frontend && npm run build` when feasible.
- Run `npm run lint` when the touched area is expected to pass current lint rules.
- For backend changes, run the lightest relevant validation available and report exactly what was verified.
- If a change touches engine-backed flows, validate the platform side and engine side separately when feasible.
- Do not claim tests ran if they did not. If no tests exist, say so and report the validation that was done.

Avoid
- Bypassing `menuArchitecture.ts` for navigation changes.
- Weakening audit, provenance, approval, or CISO gates for convenience.
- Introducing unlabeled demo data into production paths.
- Re-implementing bankability, model, or pricing logic in the platform backend when it belongs in `../gex_pf_engine/backend`.
- Touching vendored or generated folders such as `node_modules`, `venv`, or caches unless explicitly requested.
